// The `string` sub-library backs the native `string` and `char` types. It has
// no callable API: the string methods and operators are exposed by the
// language itself.
//
// Under the hood it generates `core_string.h`/`core_string.cpp`, which reuse
// the same growable `core_array::Buffer` that powers arrays. Concatenation is
// designed to keep heap allocation (and fragmentation) to a minimum:
//
// - Single contiguous block: a `String` is one character buffer backed by the
//   array `Buffer`, so there is never an allocation per character.
// - `+=`/`<<` reuse the same buffer: they append with `memcpy` into the
//   existing block and only `realloc` when capacity is insufficient, avoiding
//   a fresh `malloc` for every concatenation.
// - Template literals (`"...${expr}..."`) compile to a single accumulator
//   `String(...) << part << part`, so multi-part strings build one growing
//   buffer instead of several intermediate `String`s via `operator+`.
// - The terminator is always present, so `c_str()` is always valid; an empty
//   string is a minimal one-byte allocation, never a null pointer.
// - `push` grows geometrically (~1.25x) and `pop` shrinks by halves, matching
//   the array policy. Numeric formatting helpers live in `number_format`.
